\section{What a nonlinear body sees}{

The body of a nonlinear formula - the response formula under
\code{nl = TRUE}, or an \code{\link[=nlf]{nlf()}} body - is evaluated as a small language
for the AD tape, not as ordinary R. A function it CALLS is looked up
in RTMB first, so a bare \code{pnorm()}, \code{qgamma()}, \code{plogis()},
\code{besselK()} or \code{matrix()} in a body is RTMB's tape-capable version.
The \verb{RTMB::} prefix is no longer needed, and writing it changes
nothing: both spellings tape to the same function and give the same
fit.

RTMB WINS. It wins over the search path and over a function of the
same name that you defined yourself, because the body is a language
with its own vocabulary rather than a piece of your session. To reach
a different \code{pnorm} inside a body, qualify it: \code{stats::pnorm()} is
the escape hatch, and so is \code{myPkg::pnorm()}.

Nothing else changes. Only the names RTMB replaces are shadowed, and
only where the body calls them, so a helper function of your own, an
object the body reads, and every name that is not in RTMB still
resolve in the formula's environment exactly as before. A name the
body READS is never shadowed either, so a data frame called \code{df} or a
vector called \code{order} keeps its meaning.

The rule stops at the end-user surface. A \code{\link[=frmtmb_family]{frmtmb_family()}}
log-density and a \code{\link[=frmtmb_structure]{frmtmb_structure()}} log-likelihood are ordinary R
functions written by an extension author, and they keep resolving
lexically: qualify with \verb{RTMB::} there, and see \code{\link[=frmtmb_ad_overload]{frmtmb_ad_overload()}}
for the rest of that contract.
}

\section{Monotonic effects}{

\code{mo(x)} fits an ordinal predictor without assuming its categories are
equally spaced. \code{x} is an ordered factor or a non-negative integer
vector with at least three categories, coded \verb{0..D}. The term
contributes \code{b * D * sum(zeta[1:k])} at category \code{k}, where \code{zeta} is
a simplex of \code{D} non-negative steps summing to one. \code{b} is therefore
the AVERAGE step, on the coefficient scale of any other predictor,
and \code{zeta} says how that total is distributed over the categories.
\code{b} appears in \code{fixef()} under the term's label (\code{mox}, or \code{mox:z}
for an interaction); the simplex is held as \code{D - 1} free coordinates
in a \verb{zeta<j>} component of \code{\link[=par_template]{par_template()}}. \code{summary()} reports the
simplex itself under "Monotonic Simplex Parameters", as brms does,
with delta-method standard errors and Wald intervals held inside
\verb{[0, 1]}; \code{confint()}, \code{vcov()} and \code{start} read the coordinates,
which have no reading of their own.

The coordinates are those of stereographic projection onto the unit
sphere, whose squared components are the simplex. \code{zeta = 0} is the
uniform simplex, and every face of the simplex, where a step is 0,
is at a finite coordinate. A softmax puts a face at an infinite
coordinate, where the likelihood is flat toward it and an optimizer
stops short; this parameterization does not, so a step that is 0 at
the maximum is 0 at the estimate and keeps a finite curvature. After
the fit, a term whose simplex is on a face is also refitted with its
scale coefficient held at the other sign, because each sign of \code{b}
has its own local maximum, and the better of the two is kept
(\code{frmtmb_control(mo_search = FALSE)} turns that off). The sampler of
frmtmb.sample does not use these coordinates: it samples the softmax
under brms's default \code{dirichlet(1)} and reports brms's \code{simo_}
weights.

Every monotonic TERM gets its own simplex. \code{y ~ mo(x) * z} fits two:
one shape for the main effect and one for the interaction, because
the two terms describe different shapes and there is no reason for
the interaction to bend the way the main effect does. The simplexes
are numbered in the order \code{\link[brms:brm]{brms::brm()}} enumerates its special terms,
which is \code{stats::terms()} order and so lists every main effect before
any interaction. The j-th monotonic term of a linear predictor is
therefore the one brms calls \verb{simo_<j>} and names \verb{<label><1>} in the
\code{simo} rows of its \code{get_prior()}.

The \verb{zeta<j>} NUMBER is not that j in general. Simplexes continue the
numbering of whatever parameters the family contributed first, so
\verb{zeta<j>} is brms's \verb{simo_<j>} only for a family that declares none:
an ordinal fit spends slot 1 on its thresholds and a \code{cox()} fit on
its baseline hazard, and in both the first monotonic simplex is
\code{zeta2}. Read the terms in order rather than parsing the number.

frmtmb has no prior class for a simplex; brms's flat \code{dirichlet(1)}
is the one prior it cannot be told to drop, and it contributes only a
constant, so the two log-densities agree up to \code{lgamma(D)} per
simplex.

\code{mo()} may be crossed with a single numeric multiplier and no more:
\code{mo(x) * z} and \code{mo(x):z} are fitted, \code{mo(x) * f} for a factor \code{f} is
refused (the simplex carries one coefficient and a contrast expansion
has no column to go in), and \code{mo(x):mo(w)} is refused outright.
}

\section{Category-specific effects, cs()}{

\code{cs(x)} gives a predictor one coefficient per category boundary
instead of one for the whole response, in every ordinal family. The
term contributes an \code{n} by \code{K - 1} matrix of offsets to the
thresholds, so the effect of \code{x} on the first boundary need not be
its effect on the second. On \code{cumulative()} and \code{hurdle_cumulative()}
the offsets can make one row's thresholds cross, and the category
between two crossed thresholds then has a negative probability. brms
fits these models with a warning that the effects are experimental,
and so does this package. Such a row's density is \code{NaN}, as brms's
is; its \code{fitted()} probabilities are \code{NaN} too, where brms returns
the negative difference; and \code{simulate()} gives \code{NA} for it.
\code{predict()} reports \code{NA} proportions for a row whose thresholds cross
at the estimates, where \code{fitted()} is \code{NaN}. A row whose thresholds
cross only in some of its simulated parameter draws reports the
proportions over the draws that do not cross, and the call warns
once with the number of draws dropped per row.

A \code{cs()} term is expanded by \code{stats::model.matrix()} like any other
population-level term, which is what brms does too. A numeric
predictor is one column; a FACTOR or CHARACTER predictor is its
treatment-contrast dummies, one coefficient per dummy per boundary.
\code{cs(f)} on a three-level factor with levels \code{a}, \code{b} and \code{c} is
therefore four coefficients, named \code{fb[1]}, \code{fb[2]}, \code{fc[1]} and
\code{fc[2]} in \code{\link[=fixef]{fixef()}} and \code{bcs_fb[1]} and the rest in \code{\link[=variables]{variables()}},
with \code{a} as the reference level. \code{set_prior(class = "b")} covers them
all and \code{set_prior(class = "b", coef = "fb")} reaches one dummy's
pair; \code{\link[=default_prior]{default_prior()}} lists one row per dummy, as brms's
\code{get_prior()} does. New data are recoded against the levels and
contrasts of the FIT, so a one-row \code{newdata} whose factor carries only
the level it needs still predicts that level, and a level the fit
never saw is refused.

A column written on BOTH sides is refused. \code{y ~ x + cs(x)} is not
identified: adding a constant to the population-level coefficient of
\code{x} and subtracting the same constant from every one of its
category-specific coefficients leaves the likelihood unchanged. Write
\code{y ~ cs(x)}, which fits the same set of distributions. The same holds
for a factor on both sides (\code{y ~ f + cs(f)}) and for a constant \code{cs()}
column. brms builds both blocks and samples the ridge; maximum
likelihood has no prior to hold it, and what frmtmb reported before
the refusal was a coefficient with a standard error of 2.7e5.

The check is a RANK test, so it also catches a column that only a
basis or an arithmetic expression spells differently:
\code{y ~ I(x) + cs(x)}, \code{y ~ x1 + x2 + cs(x1 + x2)},
\code{y ~ poly(x, 2) + cs(x)} and \code{y ~ s(x) + cs(x)} are all refused, and so
are two \code{cs()} terms that are the same column under different
spellings. The rank comes from \code{qr()} at its default \code{tol = 1e-7}, over
columns scaled to unit norm, so a merely ill-conditioned design is
refused once its condition number passes about \code{1e7}. The message names
the population-level columns involved and the edit that works, which
for \code{poly(x, 2) + cs(x)} is \code{I(x^2) + cs(x)} (the same maximum, one
parameter fewer) and for \code{s(x) + cs(x)} is to drop one of the two
terms, because there it is the smooth's own unpenalized linear column
that is aliased.

The rank test cannot see an \code{mi()} or \code{me()} term: its design column is
a zero placeholder at assembly, filled later with observed-or-latent
VALUES rather than with a function of a data column, so there is no
basis to test it against. \code{mi(x) + cs(x)} and \code{me(x, sdx) + cs(x)} are
therefore accepted. \code{me(x, sdx) + cs(x)} looks genuinely identified,
its standard errors being finite, and \code{mi(x) + cs(x)} is not settled.
\code{mo()} has a placeholder column too, but whatever fills it is a
monotone function of the predictor's categories, so \code{mo()} gets a test
of its own against that category basis and \code{y ~ mo(m) + cs(m)} is
refused.

\code{cs()} is not available with \code{thres(gr = )} or under \code{importance}, and
\code{cs()} on the LEFT of a bar (\code{(cs(x) | g)}) is the covariance structure
of the same name, not a category-specific effect. A MULTIVARIATE fit
takes \code{cs()} in any response: \code{bf(yo ~ x + cs(f)) + bf(yo2 ~ z + cs(f))} fits, and the coefficients carry the response prefix,
\code{yo_fb[1]} in \code{\link[=fixef]{fixef()}} and \code{bcs_yo_fb[1]} in \code{\link[=variables]{variables()}}.

One behavior to know when a \code{cs()} level is rare. The model frame drops
an unused factor level, as brms does, so a SUBSET in which a level does
not occur is fitted with one column fewer. In \code{\link[=influence]{influence()}}, deleting
the last row of a level therefore gives a refit whose coefficients are
a subset of the full fit's: those cells stay \code{NA},
\code{\link[=cooks.distance]{cooks.distance()}} is \code{NA} for that unit, and a warning names the unit
and the coefficients.
}

\section{Ordinal thresholds, thres()}{

The four ordinal families take brms's \code{thres()} addition term.
\code{y | thres(K) ~ x} fits \code{K} thresholds, so the response has \code{K + 1}
categories even when the top ones are never observed. The data do
not place a threshold above the highest observed category: its
maximum likelihood estimate runs off toward infinity. Set a prior on
class \code{"Intercept"} to hold it, as brms's default prior does. Without
such a prior the fit warns.

\code{y | thres(gr = g) ~ x} gives each level of the factor \code{g} a
threshold vector of its own and keeps one linear predictor for all
rows. The number of thresholds of a level is the highest category
observed in that level less one, or \code{n} from \code{thres(n, gr = g)},
where \code{n} is a column that is constant within each level. These are
brms's counts. A category above the highest one a level takes is
not a category of that level, unless \code{thres(n, gr = g)} asks for it.
An ordered-factor response takes its categories from the levels that
occur in the data, as in brms, and \code{thres()} may not ask for more.
Code the response as integers to fit categories nobody chose.

The thresholds have brms's names: \code{Intercept[a,1]} in \code{fixef()} and
\code{b_Intercept[a,1]} in \code{variables()} and \code{hypothesis()}.
\code{set_prior(..., class = "Intercept", group = "a")} puts a prior on
the thresholds of level \code{a} only. As in brms, the design is not
centered under grouped thresholds.

A row in level \code{a} falls in one of the categories \verb{1..n_a + 1}.
\code{fitted()} and \code{predict()} return \code{max(n) + 1} categories, and the
probability of a category past a row's own is 0, as in brms's
\code{posterior_epred()}. \code{newdata} must hold the grouping variable, with
levels the fit has seen. With \code{gr}, \code{cs()} is refused, as in brms,
and so is \code{residuals(type = "osa")}.

A refit inside the package keeps the threshold count of the model it
came from, and keeps the count of every level of \code{thres(gr = )}.
\code{\link[=frm_bootstrap]{frm_bootstrap()}}, \code{\link[=refit]{refit()}}, \code{\link[=frm_allfit]{frm_allfit()}}, \code{anova(refit = TRUE)},
\code{confint(method = "profile")} and the autoscale pre-fit reuse the
assembled design, so the count cannot move. \code{\link[=influence]{influence()}} and
\code{cooks.distance()} rebuild the design from a subset of the data, and
carry over the count AND the response's own categories, so a subset
that empties ANY category, bottom, interior or top, still fits the
fitted model with one threshold that the subset no longer identifies.
An ordered-factor response then gives the same table as the same data
coded as integers. Two cases cannot be refit and are refused per unit,
counted and reported: a \verb{groups = } deletion that removes a whole
\code{thres(gr = )} level, and a \verb{data = } holding a response category
outside the fitted threshold layout or a \code{thres(gr = )} level the fit
never saw. A category \code{thres(K)} declared but nothing observed is
inside that layout, so \verb{data = } may reach it. See \code{\link[=influence]{influence()}}.

Calling \code{frm()} again on new data is not a refit: it counts the
thresholds of that data. That is what \code{update(newdata = )} does,
because it re-evaluates the stored call, and what \code{\link[=simulate]{simulate()}}
followed by \code{frm()} does. Write \code{thres(K)} to pin a count there.

brms's older \code{cat(x)} gives the number of CATEGORIES and is read as
\code{thres(x - 1)}, with brms's deprecation warning. \code{cat()} and
\code{thres()} together are refused, because they set one count.
}

\section{Exposures, rate()}{

\code{y | rate(d) ~ x} fits a count with exposure \code{d}, as in brms, for
\code{poisson()}, \code{negbinomial()} and \code{geometric()}. The expected count
is \code{mu * d}. Under the log link that is \code{eta + log(d)} on the
linear-predictor scale, so on \code{poisson()} the model is the same as
\code{y ~ x + offset(log(d))}. On the negative binomial brms multiplies
the shape by \code{d} too, so the variance is \verb{mu d (1 + mu / shape)},
and on \code{geometric()} the density is the negative binomial of size
\code{d}. \code{d} must be positive.

\code{\link[=fitted]{fitted()}} and \code{\link[=predict]{predict()}} report the expected count, \code{mu * d}.
\code{fitted(dpar = "mu")} and \code{\link[=frm_linpred]{frm_linpred()}} report \code{mu} and its linear
predictor without the exposure, as brms's \code{posterior_linpred()}
does. \code{newdata} must hold \code{d}. Other families refuse the term, as
brms does.
}

\section{Response subsets, subset() and index()}{

In a multivariate model, \code{y1 | subset(s) ~ x} fits \code{y1} on the rows
where the logical variable \code{s} is \code{TRUE}, so each response can have
its own rows. The design matrices and the smooth bases of a response
come from its own rows, as brms builds them from the subsetted data.
A missing value does not drop a row when every response that uses
the variable leaves that row out through its own \code{subset()}; this is
brms's rule. In a univariate model \code{subset()} is a filter on the
rows. \code{\link[=nobs]{nobs()}} counts the rows of the data, as brms's does, in a
univariate model too, and \code{nobs(fit, resp = "y1")} counts the rows
of that response in a multivariate one. The log-likelihood's own
count, which \code{BIC()} reads, is the rows of the fitted frame: the
subset's rows in a univariate model, and in a multivariate one every
row kept, including a row that no response's subset uses.

A predictor that reads another response, \code{mi(x)}, cannot line the
rows up when either response uses \code{subset()}. As in brms, name each
row of \code{x} with \code{x | mi() + index(id)} and read it with
\code{mi(x, idx = ref)}: each row of the using response takes the value
of \code{x} on the row whose \code{id} equals its \code{ref}. A value of \code{ref} with
no match, a duplicated \code{id}, and \code{mi(x)} without \code{idx} across
subsetted responses are refused with brms's messages. The
coefficient has brms's name, \code{mixidxEQref}.

\code{\link[=fitted]{fitted()}}, \code{\link[=predict]{predict()}}, \code{\link[=frm_linpred]{frm_linpred()}} and, in \code{frmtmb.sample},
\code{log_lik()} and the posterior methods need a single \verb{resp =} on such
a model, as brms asks: the responses have different rows. On
\code{newdata} they answer for the rows where that response's subset is
\code{TRUE}, and \code{newdata} must hold the subset variable. On \code{newdata},
\code{mi(x, idx = )} matches \code{idx} among the rows of \code{newdata} itself.
\code{\link[=conditional_effects]{conditional_effects()}} holds a subset variable at \code{TRUE}, as brms
does.

Refused: \code{subset()} with \code{set_rescor(TRUE)}, with \code{me()} terms (as
in brms), with \code{na.action = na.exclude}, and, in a multivariate
model, with a residual correlation term or a structured family, and
\code{vcov_cluster()} on such a model.

Three differences from brms remain, all for the same reason. brms
keeps every level of the whole data in a subsetted response, and
frmtmb keeps the levels that the response's own rows carry, so that
it creates no parameter that the data cannot place:
\itemize{
\item A grouping factor. A level with no rows adds nothing to the
marginal likelihood, so the fits agree, but \code{\link[=ranef]{ranef()}} lists
fewer levels, and \verb{|ID|}-linked terms whose rows carry different
levels are refused.
\item A factor predictor. brms keeps an all-zero column for a
level that occurs only outside the subset; frmtmb drops the
column with its rank-deficiency message, and the fit is the fit
on the subset's rows.
\item An ordinal response. A category that occurs only outside the
subset gives brms one more threshold than frmtmb, because frmtmb
counts the categories the response's own rows take.
}
}

\section{The Laplace approximation, and how to check it}{

Random effects are integrated out by the Laplace approximation,
which assumes the integrand is close to Gaussian around the
conditional mode; the Wald intervals \code{confint()} reports assume the
log-likelihood is close to quadratic at the optimum. Both degrade in
the same places: variance components estimated from few groups, and
binary data in small clusters.

Three remedies are in this package. \code{confint(method = "profile")}
replaces the quadratic assumption with a profile likelihood,
\code{\link[=frm_bootstrap]{frm_bootstrap()}} with a resampling distribution, and
\code{quadrature = TRUE} replaces the Laplace approximation itself with
adaptive quadrature (the test suite checks that fit against
\code{lme4::glmer(nAGQ = 25)} and GLMMadaptive in exactly the regime where
the Laplace fit is biased).

To MEASURE the violation rather than route around it, install the
\code{frmtmb.sample} package and call \code{frmtmb.sample::check_laplace()}. It
runs NUTS on this very objective and reports how far the posterior
mean sits from the maximum-likelihood estimate in posterior standard
deviations, and the ratio of the posterior standard deviation to the
Wald standard error. \code{vignette("diagnostics")} works through it.
}

\examples{
\dontrun{
data(sleepstudy, package = "lme4")
fit <- frm(bf(Reaction ~ Days + (Days | Subject)) + gaussian(),
              data = sleepstudy)
summary(fit)
}
set.seed(1)
dd <- data.frame(x = rnorm(100), g = factor(rep(1:10, 10)))
dd$y <- rnorm(100, 1 + 0.5 * dd$x + rnorm(10, 0, 0.5)[dd$g], 1)
fit <- frm(bf(y ~ x + (1 | g)) + gaussian(), data = dd)
summary(fit)
fixef(fit)
VarCorr(fit)

# distributional regression: model sigma too
fit2 <- frm(bf(y ~ x + (1 | g), sigma ~ x) + gaussian(), data = dd)
anova(fit, fit2)

# a nonlinear model: discover the names, edit, fit. A nonlinear body
# is undefined at the zero start, so `start` is not optional here.
nd <- data.frame(t = rep(seq(0, 10, length.out = 25), 4))
nd$y <- 8 * (1 - exp(-nd$t / 3)) + rnorm(nrow(nd), 0, 0.3)
nf <- bf(y ~ asym * (1 - exp(-t / lrc)), asym ~ 1, lrc ~ 1,
         nl = TRUE) + gaussian()
st <- par_template(nf, data = nd)
st
st$beta[["asym_(Intercept)"]] <- 5
st$beta[["lrc_(Intercept)"]] <- 1
fixef(frm(nf, data = nd, start = st))

# or let a located prior place the same starts, brms-style
fixef(frm(nf, data = nd,
          prior = prior(normal(5, 5), nlpar = "asym") +
            prior(normal(1, 5), nlpar = "lrc")))
}
